WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:07.261
‘We added an agent.’ That is not yet a claim.

2
00:00:07.261 --> 00:00:14.505
Inside a service, a language-model call can be arranged in thirteen shapes, and they are different things.

3
00:00:14.505 --> 00:00:26.436
This episode names them, places each in the drone-delivery estate, and pairs it with the microservice decision it most resembles — with one warning that repeats on purpose.

4
00:00:27.036 --> 00:00:29.361
Two sources carry the names.

5
00:00:29.361 --> 00:00:40.986
Anthropic’s ‘Building effective agents’, from December 2024, separates workflows — where code paths are fixed — from agents, where the model directs its own tools.

6
00:00:40.986 --> 00:00:47.496
The Azure Architecture Center’s orchestration guide puts the same work on a complexity ladder.

7
00:00:47.496 --> 00:00:55.402
Both start from one rule: use the smallest structure that meets the requirement. A direct model call.

8
00:00:55.402 --> 00:00:58.192
Then one augmented model with tools.

9
00:00:58.192 --> 00:01:10.282
Then, only when one call is unreliable, one agent needs too many tools, or two parts of the work need different security boundaries — multi-agent orchestration.

10
00:01:10.282 --> 00:01:14.932
It is not justified by the presence of a framework.

11
00:01:15.532 --> 00:01:18.950
A direct model call: one prompt, one response.

12
00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:27.494
In the estate, the model rewrites a delay notice — it does not decide that the delivery may be cancelled,

13
00:01:27.494 --> 00:01:33.475
and a call that is allowed to invent the window has left the pattern.

14
00:01:33.475 --> 00:01:39.883
The augmented LLM: one model that retrieves, calls tools, and remembers, inside an iteration limit.

15
00:01:39.883 --> 00:01:49.282
Its tools are outbound ports to owning services — ‘get delivery status’ is fine; ‘update the deliveries table’ crosses the database-per-service line.

16
00:01:49.282 --> 00:01:55.690
The quality of the system sits in the tools as much as in the prompt.

17
00:01:55.690 --> 00:02:03.380
And retrieval-augmented generation — named by Lewis and colleagues in 2020 — fetches passages, then answers from them.

18
00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:12.779
The index is a read model, fed by a published language; if it copies every service’s tables, it is a shared database.

19
00:02:12.779 --> 00:02:19.187
Tenant trimming happens at retrieval, not after. The model is not the system of record.

20
00:02:19.787 --> 00:02:32.519
Prompt chaining: a fixed sequence of calls, each taking the last output, with a programmatic gate between steps — draft the notice, check it names the right window, shorten it, translate it.

21
00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:44.456
A chain is not yet a saga; the moment a step charges a card, that step is a command on the owning service and needs an outbox and a compensation.

22
00:02:44.456 --> 00:02:56.790
Routing: classify the input once, up front, and send it down a prepared path — the categories are the bounded contexts, and a wrong class means the aggregate’s rule never runs.

23
00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:01.565
Parallelization: independent calls run together and joined, by sectioning or by voting.

24
00:03:01.565 --> 00:03:11.114
It resembles API composition, except that judgments can conflict without either call having failed — so the join policy is part of the pattern.

25
00:03:11.114 --> 00:03:23.051
And voting on whether a delivery may be cancelled is the wrong use: that rule is on the entity, and a vote of models would replace it with a tally.

26
00:03:23.651 --> 00:03:34.031
Orchestrator-workers: a central model invents the subtasks at runtime and synthesizes the results — saga orchestration whose steps are proposed per input.

27
00:03:34.031 --> 00:03:44.883
Fine for a question over an unknown set of read-only sources; wrong for ‘cancel and refund’, whose steps are known and have compensations.

28
00:03:44.883 --> 00:03:51.017
The pattern chooses the calls. It does not receive a private write path.

29
00:03:51.017 --> 00:03:57.623
Evaluator-optimizer, or maker-checker: one model drafts, another critiques against explicit criteria, until a cap.

30
00:03:57.623 --> 00:04:10.362
Good for a notice that has not been sent; useless once a fact is committed — criticism is for an artifact still inside the AI system’s boundary.

31
00:04:10.362 --> 00:04:16.024
Group chat: several agents in one thread under a manager, typically read-only.

32
00:04:16.024 --> 00:04:21.686
The thread is an audit log; the manager should be the writer.

33
00:04:21.686 --> 00:04:33.010
Handoff: one active agent transfers the whole task when it meets a limit — capped transfers, state carried by identity, and no privilege upgrade.

34
00:04:33.010 --> 00:04:43.390
And magentic orchestration, from Microsoft’s Magentic-One: a manager keeps a task ledger, replans when progress stalls, and sends workers at external systems.

35
00:04:43.390 --> 00:04:51.411
The ledger is a plan; a saga log is history. The ledger does not replace that history.

36
00:04:52.011 --> 00:05:02.141
The autonomous agent: the model directs its own process and tools until the task ends, a person stops it, or a turn limit is reached.

37
00:05:02.141 --> 00:05:11.866
Its ground truth is the tool result — a model that treats a timeout as ‘the delivery must be cancelled’ has ignored the environment.

38
00:05:11.866 --> 00:05:20.375
It lives most safely inside one capability, with tools that are queries and drafts; the aggregate rules stay in the services.

39
00:05:20.375 --> 00:05:29.290
And the human checkpoint: a designed stop, scoped to the sensitive tool — summarize without approval, refund only after a person approves.

40
00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:33.342
The approval is an authorization decision, recorded as audit logging.

41
00:05:33.342 --> 00:05:39.015
The pending action is a business state of the service that will perform it.

42
00:05:39.015 --> 00:05:43.067
A checkpoint that nobody can see is not the pattern.

43
00:05:43.667 --> 00:05:52.759
These patterns are control structures for model calls. The microservice baseline is still the control structure for business facts.

44
00:05:52.759 --> 00:05:58.023
A prompt chain does not become a saga by having steps.

45
00:05:58.023 --> 00:06:07.593
A retrieval index does not become the owner of a delivery by holding passages about it. Similarity is not identity.

46
00:06:07.593 --> 00:06:17.163
And what an implementation still owes the baseline is the baseline’s own list: timeouts, retries, and breakers on model calls;

47
00:06:17.163 --> 00:06:26.734
validated output; checkpointed state; logs, metrics, and traces per call and per handoff; doubles for tools and rubrics for orchestrations.

48
00:06:26.734 --> 00:06:30.084
None of that moves the service boundary.

49
00:06:30.684 --> 00:06:37.550
A claim can now name a pattern in this list and a decision in the baseline, separately.

50
00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:44.821
A direct call inside notification; a retrieval index owned by delivery; a router in front of existing contexts;

51
00:06:44.821 --> 00:06:52.899
a saga whose coordinator is still designed by hand; and a magentic ledger that can refund without the accounts service.

52
00:06:52.899 --> 00:07:01.381
Five different claims. Only the last has moved a business boundary — and it has moved it by bypassing the owner.

53
00:07:01.381 --> 00:07:10.671
Grade a tool by which pattern it implements, which tools can write, who owns the passages it retrieves, and what stops the loop.

54
00:07:11.271 --> 00:07:20.618
Three things to carry away. One. Climb the ladder only as far as the requirement demands. Two.

55
00:07:20.618 --> 00:07:28.865
An AI pattern and a microservice pattern are two columns; similarity is not identity. Three.

56
00:07:28.865 --> 00:07:35.462
The model proposes; the owning service decides; a person approves the irreversible.

57
00:07:36.062 --> 00:07:41.421
The last part of the series — what AI actually changed — is still being written.

58
00:07:41.421 --> 00:07:46.110
When it is, it will be graded by every rule you have just heard.
